
;;
	a procedure definition is written as:
	name:( args : rets ) { body }

	args and rets list the types the procedure consumes
	from the stack and returns to it
	an empty list means no values on that side
	the : may be omitted if there are no return values

	the body must consume the arguments and leave exactly
	the return values on the stack, in the order declared

	a procedure is called by writing its name followed by #
	the arguments must already be on the stack when the call
	is made, and the return values will be on the stack when
	it finishes

	definitions do not need to appear before their first
	use; the compiler resolves the name across the whole
	compilation unit

	execution starts at the entry point
	the entry point is user-defined
	by default the compiler looks for a procedure called
	'main', but this can be changed with a compiler flag
;;

foo:(:s4){ 35 }

main:() {
     foo# bar# + dmp
}

bar:(:s4) { 34 }